From 1cb185325055aae7435c6f5984fe13cd400e28b6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: leow149 <leow149@lucidmail.xyz>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 01:20:21 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: xradio: start AP with the current channel width

wsm_start() always sent the 20 MHz PHY mode word. With a station joined at
HT40 on the same channel, starting the AP on the second vif retuned the
firmware to 20 MHz; the station stayed associated but stopped passing
traffic until the AP was removed and the station reassociated.

Use wsm_phy_mode_cur(), the same chandef-derived word the join uses, so both
vifs run on the shared channel and width.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JJfik1hpStPgtN7deJRwXG
Signed-off-by: leow149 <leow149@lucidmail.xyz>
---
 drivers/net/wireless/xradio/wsm.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/xradio/wsm.c b/drivers/net/wireless/xradio/wsm.c
index b7e313a14..6ab5815c3 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/xradio/wsm.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/xradio/wsm.c
@@ -1011,7 +1011,7 @@ int wsm_start(struct xradio_common *hw_priv, const struct wsm_start *arg,
 	wsm_cmd_lock(hw_priv);
 
 	if (xradio_ht40_api(hw_priv)) {
-		WSM_PUT16(buf, wsm_phy_mode_20mhz(arg->band));
+		WSM_PUT16(buf, wsm_phy_mode_cur(hw_priv, arg->band));
 		WSM_PUT16(buf, arg->channelNumber);
 		WSM_PUT32(buf, arg->CTWindow);
 		WSM_PUT32(buf, arg->beaconInterval);
